{{also|runup|run-up}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|en-au-run up.ogg|a=AU}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|run<,,ran,run> up}}

# To run (towards someone or something); to hasten to a destination.
#: {{ux|en|As I was walking along the road, a man suddenly '''ran up''' to me.}}
#: {{ux|en|The dog '''ran up''' under the table to get his food.}}
# {{lb|en|with ''to''}} To [[approach]] (an event or point in time).
#: {{ux|en|We are putting on lots of special attractions as we '''run up''' to Christmas.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To take to a destination or before an authority.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1924|author=Michigan. Supreme Court|title=Michigan Reports|volume=226|page=46
|passage={{...}} and I took him along and '''ran''' him '''up''' to police headquarters.}}
# {{senseid|en|erect hastily}} To erect hastily, as a building.
#: {{syn|en|throw up<id:to erect, especially hastily><qq:dated>|knock up|knock together|slap together}}
#* {{quote-journal |en |year=1840 |month=February |last=Poe |first=Edgar Allan |authorlink=Edgar Allan Poe |title=Peter Pendulum, the Business Man |journal=Burton's Gentleman's Magazine |volume=6 |issue=2 |page=87 |isbn= |url=https://www.eapoe.org/works/tales/bumana.htm |doi= |passage=we wait until the palace is half-way up, and then we pay some tasty architect to '''run''' us '''up''' an ornamental mud hovel, right against it}}
# {{lb|en|idiomatic|transitive}} To [[make]] something, usually an item of clothing, very [[quickly]].
#: {{ux|en|I'll '''run''' you '''up''' a skirt for tomorrow evening.}}
# {{lb|en|idiomatic|transitive}} To bring (a [[flag]]) to the top of its [[flag pole]].
#: {{ux|en|Stand quietly while the honor guard '''runs''' the flag '''up'''.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[string up]]; to [[hang]].
# {{lb|en|cricket}} Of a [[bowler]], to run, or [[walk]] up to the [[bowling crease]] in order to [[bowl]] a [[ball]].
#: {{ux|en|He '''runs up'''... and bowls. Smashed away for four runs!}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|transitive}} To [[rise]]; to swell; to grow; to [[increase]].
#: {{ux|en|Accounts of goods credited '''run up''' very fast.}}
#* {{RQ:Scott Kenilworth|passage=But these, having been untrimmed for many years, had '''run up''' into great bushes, or rather dwarf trees.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|url=https://archive.nytimes.com/gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2009/02/06/google-glitch-mayrun-up-phone-bills/|title=Google Glitch May Run Up Phone Bills|date=2009-02-06|author=Roy Furchgott|work=New York Times|passage=Google Glitch May '''Run Up''' Phone Bills [title]}}
# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} To [[accumulate]] (a [[debt]]).
#: {{ux|en|He '''ran up''' over $5,000 in unpaid bills.}}
# To [[thrust]] up, as anything long and slender.
#: {{ux|en|The fence '''runs up''' along the edge of the pasture.}}
# {{lb|en|aviation|transitive}} To [[warm up]] and test an airplane before a flight.
# {{lb|en|AAVE|slang|sometimes reflexive}} To accumulate money, drugs, etc.
#: {{ux|en|I '''run me up''' some big bills.}}
# {{&lit|en|run|up}}
#: {{ux|en|The small boy '''ran up''' the hill.}}

